oming across, focus on the message you want to convey. What do you want your audience to take away from your presentation? What key points do you want to emphasize?\n3. **Make eye contact**: As your daughter has pointed out, making eye contact is essential for engaging with your audience. Try to make eye contact with different people in the room, and hold their gaze for a few seconds before moving on to the next person.\n4. **Vary your pace**: Speaking too fast can make it difficult for your audience to follow along. Try to vary your pace to keep your audience engaged. Pause for emphasis, slow down for complex information, and speed up for more exciting or energetic parts of your presentation.\n5. **Use body language**: Your body language can convey just as much information as your words. Make sure to stand up straight, use gestures to emphasize your points, and move around the room to engage with your audience.\n6. **Prepare for questions**: Anticipate questions your audience might ask and prepare answers in advance. This will help you feel more confident and in control during the Q&A session.\n7. **Get familiar with the presentation space**: If possible, visit the presentation space before your presentation to get a feel for the room and the equipment. This will help you feel more comfortable and prepared on the day of the presentation.\n8. **Take care of yourself**: Make sure to get plenty of rest, eat well, and exercise before your presentation. Taking care of your physical and mental health will help you feel more confident and focused.\n9. **Use positive self-talk**: Instead of focusing on your nerves, try to focus on positive affirmations. Tell yourself that you're well-prepared, that you have valuable information to share, and that you're capable of delivering a great presentation.\n10. **Seek feedback**: Continue to seek feedback from your family, friends, or colleagues. Ask them to provide specific feedback on areas you need to improve, and use that feedback to make adjustments to your presentation.\n\nRemember, the key to improving your public speaking skills is to practice consistently and be open to feedback. With time and practice, you'll become more confident and comfortable speaking in front of an audience.\n\nHow do you feel about your presentation so far? Is there anything specific that's making you nervous, or any areas you'd like to focus on improving?\nuser: I'm feeling a bit more confident now, thanks for the tips. I think I'll focus on making more eye contact and varying my pace. I've also been thinking about using some visual aids to help illustrate my points. Do you have any advice on how to create effective visual aids?\nassistant: Visual aids can be a great way to enhance your presentation and help your audience",
