roviding, rather than your own anxiety.\n2. **Take deep breaths**: Before you go on stage, take a few deep breaths to calm your nerves. This will help slow down your heart rate and reduce your anxiety.\n3. **Use positive self-talk**: Encourage yourself with positive affirmations, such as \"I've prepared well, and I can do this.\"\n\n**Improve your delivery:**\n\n1. **Make eye contact**: Engage with your audience by making eye contact with different people in the room. This will help you connect with them and build trust.\n2. **Vary your tone and pace**: Use inflection, pauses, and changes in pace to keep your audience engaged. Avoid monotone delivery, which can be boring and hard to follow.\n3. **Use body language**: Make use of gestures, posture, and facial expressions to convey confidence and enthusiasm. Avoid fidgeting, crossing your arms, or leaning on the podium.\n\n**Learn from your mistakes:**\n\n1. **Reflect on your presentation**: After your presentation, take time to reflect on what went well and what didn't. Identify areas for improvement and make a plan to work on them.\n2. **Seek feedback**: Ask for feedback from others, whether it's from the audience, a mentor, or a colleague. Use this feedback to improve your skills and adjust your approach.\n3. **Don't be too hard on yourself**: Remember that everyone makes mistakes, and it's okay to stumble over your words or make errors. Use these experiences as opportunities to learn and grow.\n\n**Additional tips:**\n\n1. **Join a public speaking group**: Consider joining a group like Toastmasters, which provides a supportive environment to practice public speaking and receive feedback.\n2. **Watch TED Talks**: Study the delivery and style of experienced speakers, such as TED Talk speakers. Analyze what makes their presentations engaging and effective.\n3. **Focus on your message**: Remember that your message is more important than your nervousness. Focus on sharing your knowledge and insights with the audience, 